  "slug": "drug-barriers-2190",
  "summary": "Recent papers pair multi-layer omics with genetic causal inference to localize candidate targets to specific cell populations and molecular layers.",
  "synthesis": "The dominant thread is the pairing of multi-layer omics with genetic causal inference to nominate candidate genes and targets. Several items apply Mendelian randomization with colocalization at cell-level resolution. The vitiligo study nominates LIPA in monocytes and LTA4H in dendritic cells using single-cell eQTL data, and the TRDMT1 breast cancer study links T-cell expression to immunotherapy response and prognosis using single-cell and spatial transcriptomics. The gastric cancer study integrates GWAS with mQTL, eQTL and pQTL data via SMR and colocalization to prioritize ER stress genes including PCSK6, YOD1 and RNF5. What is distinct is that causal inference localizes a candidate effect to a specific population or molecular layer. Other items, such as the keratoconus study combining single-cell data with machine learning, are more descriptive in scope and remain hypothesis-generating.",
  "synthesis_model": "claude-haiku-5-5",
  "synthesized_on": "2026-10-27"
}
